#
# An undecidable condition nested one level inside the rule that gates another rule.
#
# `Enabled` is a boolean, so `!EMPTY` on it has no answer. That clause sits inside a `when` block in
# the body of `inner_gate`, and `inner_gate` is the condition of `guarded`. The question is what two
# levels of conversion do to the violation `guarded` is supposed to catch.
#
# Reported by a reviewer against the first version of the incompatible-type fix, and the diagnosis
# was exact: the undecidable answer was converted to `Status::FAIL` at the nested `when`, and one
# level out a FAIL on a condition is indistinguishable from a condition that was decided and did not
# match, so `eval_rule` reported the rule as not applicable and exited 0 with `MustBeTrue` unchecked.
# The merge-base exited 19 here, for the wrong reason: `!EMPTY` on a boolean was unconditionally true
# there, so the gate opened and the body ran.
#
# The single-level form of this is `unevaluatable_gate_guarding_a_violation.guard`. Both are needed:
# that one fails closed through the rule's own condition, this one through a condition reached across
# a parameterized-rule boundary, and the second path was open while the first was closed.
#
rule inner_gate(unused) {
    when Enabled !EMPTY {
        Enabled == true
    }
}

rule guarded when inner_gate("x") {
    MustBeTrue == true
}
